NAME
       ln - make hard links or symbolic links

SYNOPSIS
       ln [-s] [-f] [-v] [-r] [-n] TARGET LINKNAME
       ln [-s] [-f] [-v] TARGET... DIRECTORY
       ln [-s] -t DIRECTORY TARGET...

DESCRIPTION
       ln makes a new name for TARGET. Without -s it makes a hard link, with
       -s a symbolic (soft) link. When the last argument is a directory, the
       links are made inside it with the targets' names.

   Hard links
       A file's data lives in an inode; a directory entry is just a name that
       points to it. A hard link is one more such name. Both names are equal:
       there is no "original", the data stays until the last name is
       removed, and ls -l shows the number of names (the link count) in the
       second column. ls -i shows the inode number, which is the same for
       both.

       Hard links work only inside one file system, and not for
       directories.

   Symbolic links
       A symbolic link is a small file that holds a path. Opening it opens
       whatever is at that path. It may point to a directory or to another
       file system, and it breaks (dangles) if the target is moved or
       removed. ls -l shows it as l...  name -> target.

       The path is stored as you type it. A relative target is read from
       the link's own directory, not from where you ran ln, so ln -s
       ../data/file /srv/link points to /srv/../data/file. When in doubt,
       give an absolute target, or use -r.

OPTIONS
       -s, --symbolic
              Make a symbolic link.

       -f, --force
              Replace an existing LINKNAME.

       -v, --verbose
              Print each link made.

       -r, --relative
              With -s, store the target as a path relative to the link.

       -n, --no-dereference
              If LINKNAME is a symbolic link to a directory, replace it
              (with -f) rather than create the link inside the directory.

       -T, --no-target-directory
              Always treat LINKNAME as the name of the link.

       -t DIR, --target-directory=DIR
              Make the links in DIR.

EXAMPLES
       A hard link, and how to see that it is one:

           ln /root/data.txt /root/data-link.txt
           ls -li /root/data*.txt

       A symbolic link to a directory:

           ln -s /var/log/httpd /root/weblogs

       Point an existing link somewhere else:

           ln -sfn /srv/release-2 /srv/current

       Find every hard link to a file:

           find / -samefile /root/data.txt 2>/dev/null

SEE ALSO
       link(1), readlink(1), ls(1), find(1), rm(1)
